---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="About Erick Platero — ASIC Engineer at Qualcomm">
  <div class="about-hero">
    <img src="/assets/images/bio_photo_2_cropped_2.jpg" alt="Erick Platero" />
    <div class="about-hero-text">
      <h1>Erick Platero</h1>
      <p>ASIC Engineer · Qualcomm Cloud AI 100 · Austin, TX</p>
      <div class="about-links">
        <a href="https://github.com/eplatero97" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/erick-platero-482157214/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I'm an ASIC Engineer on Qualcomm's Cloud AI 100 team, where I work at the intersection of
      hardware and software to ship LLM inference at scale. My day-to-day spans developing the
      <a href="https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vLLM</a> QAIC
      backend, integrating Speculative Decoding techniques (vanilla, PLD, turbo) from prototype to
      production, and leading <strong>Valiant</strong> — our internal post-silicon validation
      framework built on Python, Jenkins, and MongoDB. I also led benchmark gathering for a private
      MLPerf v4.1 submission.
    </p>

    <p>
      On the research side, I recently published <strong>Greedy Tree Attention</strong> (Feb 2025),
      a compute-friendly tree attention implementation for detached Speculative Decoding that showed
      a 16% improvement over greedy Speculative Decoding.
    </p>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>
      I hold an MSc in Computer Science from the University of Houston (GPA 3.7) and a BSc in
      Data Science from UH-Downtown (GPA 3.9). Before Qualcomm, I interned at H-E-B as a Business
      Systems Analyst, where I built an automated QA framework that cut test time by 80% and
      prototyped a computer vision system for their Smart Customer Alert platform.
    </p>

    <p>
      This blog started as a way to document deep learning concepts I was learning — implementing
      layers, activation functions, and optimizers from scratch. That curiosity about how things
      work under the hood is what eventually led me to the hardware side of AI.
    </p>

    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ML Serving & Optimization:</strong> vLLM, PyTorch, ONNX, Speculative Decoding</li>
      <li><strong>Agentic AI:</strong> MCP, crewAI</li>
      <li><strong>HW Validation:</strong> Python, Jenkins, MongoDB</li>
      <li><strong>Observability:</strong> Prometheus, Grafana, Docker</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Outside of work</h2>
    <p>
      Soccer, frisbee, camping, reading, and experimenting with technology at small scale. I also
      enjoy spending time with my wife.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
